Since _R_<1, the approximation 26.3.25 is used. This results in

\[P(R^{2}|2,r^{2}) = \frac{2(.54)^{2}}{4+(.54)^{2}}\exp\frac{-2(1.21)^{2}}{4+(.54)^{2}}\] \[= (.1359)\,e^{-.682} = .06869\]

The answer to 5D is .06870.

**Example 11.** Find _Q_(25.298|20) using the interpolation formula given with Table 26.7.

Taking x2=25, th=.298 and applying the interpolation formula results in

\[\begin{array}{l} Q(25.298|20) = \frac{1}{8}(\,Q(25|16)\theta^{2} + Q(25|18)\,(4\theta-2\theta^{2})\\ + Q(25|20)(8-4\theta+\theta^{2})\,)\\ = \frac{1}{8}(\,(.06982)(.088804)\\ + (.12492)\,(1.014392)\\ + (.20143)\,(6.896804)\,)\\ = .19027\end{array}\]

A less accurate interpolate may be obtained by setting th equal to zero in the above formula. This results in the value .19003. The correct value to 6D is _Q_(25.298|20) = .190259.

On the other hand if x2=25.298 is assumed to have an error of \(\pm 5\times 10^{-4}\), then how large an error arises in _Q_(x2|_n_)? Denoting the error in x2 by D_x2 and the resulting error in _Q_(x2|_n_) by D_Q_(x2|_n_), we then have the approximate relationship

\[\Delta Q(x^{2}|_{p}) \approx \frac{\partial Q(x^{2}|_{p})}{\partial x^{2}}\,\Delta x^{2}\]

Using 26.4.8 we can write

\[\frac{\partial Q(x^{2}|_{p})}{\partial x^{2}} = \frac{1}{2}[\,Q(\chi^{2}|_{p}-2)-Q(\chi^{2}|_{p})\,]\]

and

\[\Delta Q(\chi^{2}|_{p}) \approx \frac{1}{2}[\,Q(\chi^{2}|_{p}-2)-Q(\chi^{2}|_{p})\,]\Delta x^{2}\]

For practical purposes it is sufficient to evaluate the derivative to one or two significant figures. Consequently we can write

\[\frac{\partial Q(x^{2}|_{p})}{\partial x^{2}} \approx \frac{\partial Q(x^{2}_{0}|_{p})}{\partial x^{2}}\]

where x2 is the closest value to x2 for which \(Q\) is tabulated. Hence

\[\Delta Q(\chi^{2}|_{p}) = \frac{1}{2}[\,Q(\chi^{2}|_{p}-2)-Q(\chi^{2}|_{p})\,]\Delta x^{2}\]

For this example D_x2= +-5x10-4 and x2=25. This results in

\[\Delta Q(\chi^{2}|_{p}) = \frac{1}{2}(\,-.076)\,(\pm 5)\,10^{-4} = \pm 2\times 10^{-8}\]

as the possible error in _Q_(x2|_n_).

